! The same silicon on an **unshifted** 4x4x4 grid, reduced to its wedge.
!
! Half of a pair. An unshifted Monkhorst-Pack grid *is* closed under the point
! group -- every one of the 3072 rotation images of this one lands back on it --
! so the same k-sample can be run two ways: reduced to 8 points with the
! response symmetrised (this file), or whole at 64 points with no symmetry at
! all (si-epsilon-unshifted-nosym.in). The two must give the same dielectric
! tensor, and nothing is shared between the two routes except the Sternheimer
! solve, so agreeing is the check on `symmetrize_directional`.
!
! It is *not* the sampling QE's benchmark uses, and its dielectric constant is
! nowhere near converged (23.6 against 13.8): an unshifted grid includes Gamma,
! where silicon's gap is smallest and the response largest, and 4^3 points do
! not average that away. That is a property of the k-sample, not of the method.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.20, nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 18.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
